      "title": "Understand the True Cost of Delivery",
      "paragraphs": [
        "2.1 Stop inventing prices line by line\nA weak pricing process often looks like this:\n1. A prospect describes what they want.\n2. The salesperson improvises a list of line items.\n3. Prices are assigned from memory or intuition.\n4. A total is assembled.\n5. The quote is emailed.\n6. The prospect returns with questions or changes.\n7. The team recalculates the quote manually.\nThis creates inconsistent pricing, slow proposals, and limited visibility into project profitability.",
        "A better process starts with the actual cost of delivering different levels of service.\n2.2 Separate fixed and variable costs\nFixed delivery costs do not change directly with every additional customer or unit of project volume within the relevant capacity range.",
        "Examples:\n- A project manager's allocated time.\n- A minimum staffing requirement.\n- Software subscriptions.\n- A base production or preparation cost.\n- A fixed planning fee paid to a supplier.\nVariable costs change as the project becomes larger or more complex.",
        "Examples:\n- Per-attendee catering.\n- Per-person transport.\n- Additional contractors.\n- Extra equipment.\n- More vendor coordination.\n- Additional production hours.\n- Accommodation or logistics that scale with headcount.\nSome costs are semi-variable: they remain stable until capacity thresholds are crossed, then increase in steps. For example, one coordinator may manage up to a defined number of attendees, while a larger event requires a second coordinator.\n2.3 Build a cost model for each service level\nA practical cost model might contain:\n| Cost category | Cost behaviour | Example input |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Project management | Fixed or step-fixed | Hours allocated to the project |\n| Core team payroll | Fixed or step-fixed | Staff capacity consumed |\n| Contractors | Variable | Number of contractors × rate |\n| Materials and equipment | Variable | Units required × unit cost |\n| Travel and accommodation | Variable | Actual expected expense |\n| Venue or supplier costs | Variable or pass-through | Supplier quote |\n| Contingency | Risk allowance | Defined percentage or amount |\n| Rework allowance | Risk-adjusted | Based on historical experience |",
        "For each project, estimate the quantity of each input and calculate the total expected delivery cost.\n2.4 Allocate employee capacity honestly\nA salaried employee is not free simply because the salary is already being paid.",
        "If a team member spends one quarter of their available delivery capacity on a project, the cost model should reflect that allocation. Otherwise, the business may understate the cost of delivery and overstate the project's profitability.",
        "For example, if an employee's fully loaded annual employment cost is $60,000 and one project consumes 25% of their annual capacity, the project should carry an appropriate share of that cost. The exact allocation method should match how the business tracks time and capacity.\n2.5 Calculate project contribution\nA simple starting point:\nGross Profit = Revenue - Direct Delivery Costs\nGross Margin (%) = (Gross Profit ÷ Revenue) × 100\nFor a more useful project-level view, also subtract variable selling and fulfilment costs:\nContribution = Revenue - Direct Costs - Variable Selling Costs\nDo not mix gross margin, contribution margin, and net profit. They answer different questions.\n2.6 Create a dynamic quote calculator\nThe calculator should accept the information gathered during discovery and return a consistent estimate.",
        "Possible inputs:\n- Customer or project type.\n- Number of attendees, users, locations, or deliverables.\n- Service tier.\n- Number of vendors or subcontractors.\n- Required staff and hours.\n- Complexity level.\n- Travel or location requirements.\n- Optional add-ons.\n- Timeline and urgency.\n- Scope assumptions.\nThe calculator should produce:\n- Estimated direct cost.\n- Allocated internal labour.\n- Contingency.\n- Recommended price range.\n- Expected gross profit and margin.\n- Scope boundaries.\n- Payment schedule.\n- Any approval required for unusual pricing.\nThis can be built in a spreadsheet first. A small internal application can come later if the process is stable. AI can help write formulas or build the interface, but the owner must validate the assumptions.\n2.7 A sample pricing calculator structure\n| Input | Example | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Base project cost | $8,000 | Minimum delivery requirement |\n| Variable cost per attendee | $35 | Supplier and fulfilment cost |\n| Attendees | 500 | Discovery input |\n| Additional contractor cost | $2,000 | Based on staffing threshold |\n| Allocated internal labour | $3,000 | Capacity consumed |\n| Contingency | $1,500 | Based on risk |\n| Estimated delivery cost | $32,000 | Sum of cost components |",
        "The figures are illustrative only. The model should use the business's own supplier rates, employee costs, historical overruns, and actual delivery data."
      ]
